From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jonathan Schwender <schwenderjonathan@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 12:29:25 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] script: Close a shared worker once its last owner document is
 gone

Servo kept no owner set for shared workers: a shared worker ran until it
closed itself, even after every document that used it was gone. Its
IndexedDB connections stayed open with it, so an upgrade or delete of
their database by a later page waited for them forever. In WPT, a
.any.sharedworker variant whose subtest failed inside upgradeneeded left
its connection open, and the .any.worker variant of the same test, run
after it in the same browser, timed out (idbobjectstore-rename-store).

The script-side shared worker registry now keeps each worker's owner set
(https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/#the-worker's-lifetime) as the
pipelines of its owner documents; Servo only lets windows construct
shared workers. The constructor adds its document when it finds the
worker (step 11.5.8, under the registry lock together with the lookup,
so the worker can't be closed as an orphan in between) or when the
worker it started is registered ("run a worker"); a constructor that
then fails to connect takes out an owner it added. Destroying a document
(step 10 of "destroy a document"), or the end of a window whose pipeline
exits, removes it from every owner set. When a set becomes empty, the
worker is closed as an orphan: its closing flag is set and the exit
control message wakes its event loop, whose end runs the IndexedDB
teardown. A worker with extendedLifetime is closed only if its owner set
is still empty after 30 seconds (the extended lifetime shared worker
timeout; Chromium keeps a service worker without clients that long).
Servo keeps no worker for the between-loads timeout.

The extended lifetime runs on a timer of the worker's own event loop,
which the control message CloseIfStillOrphanedAfterTimeout schedules,
not on a thread per orphaned worker that slept for 30 seconds and could
outlive the browser's shutdown (P5 review N7).

Validation: build host SM and QuickJS release and run /_mozilla/IndexedDB/shared-worker-owners.html with --repeat 3 (new: the only owner removed closes the worker's connection, a second owner keeps it, a constructor with mismatched options leaves the owner set as it was): 3 of 3 subtests 3 of 3 on both engines, while 92b5f086e08 + the wake-up commit times out the first; the rename-store pair in one browser with the sharedworker variant first (P5.2/scripts/pair.sh, PYTHONHASHSEED 1 and 2, --repeat 3): worker variant TIMEOUT 6 of 6 before, 0 of 6 now; host SM IndexedDB/ 4,483 of 4,511 with no unexpected results, /_mozilla/IndexedDB/ 309 of 309; /workers/ and html/browsers/browsing-the-web/back-forward-cache/ without unexpected results on both engines (SM 1,536 of 1,581 as with 92b5f086e08); after the P5 review fix (the worker's own timer): host SM release builds, 151 passed, 1 skipped at this commit; /_mozilla/IndexedDB/shared-worker-owners.html and /workers/ with every result as expected on the stack tip
AI-assisted: Yes (agent session; the agent implemented the owner set and the orphan closing, wrote the fork test and measured it on both engines; the P5 review fix moved the extended lifetime onto the worker's own timer)
Signed-off-by: Jonathan Schwender <schwenderjonathan@gmail.com>
